  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 PM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nPoor mixing with easterly flow in place has allowed some low clouds \nto hold on across western and southern portions of the forecast area \nthis afternoon. Continued southeast progression of the southeast US \nupper trough will allow background upper ridging across central \nCONUS to eventually build eastward across the western Great Lakes \nand Ohio Valley through Saturday. This evolutions should allow low \nclouds to diminish this evening as much drier low level air\nadvects in from the east. This should leave mostly clear/partly\ncloudy skies developing this evening with low clouds having\nlongest residence time across NW Indiana. In addition, some \nincrease in high level clouds is expected from west to east \nlater tonight into early Friday. However, arrival time of this\nthicker cloud cover should still be late enough for most \nlocations to dip into the 40s. Warmest temps are expected across\nfar northwest Indiana where low clouds will have some extra \nresidence time tonight along with a quicker replacement by \nhigher cloud cover. This could keep lows closer to 50 or in the\nlower 50s for these locations.\n\nVery little progression in the mid/upper level pattern is expected \nFriday through Sunday with a stable longwave pattern developing that \nwill feature primary short wave storm track across southwest Canada. \nThe upper ridge axis should remain displaced west of the local area \nthrough the weekend which will keep a broad low level anticyclone \nanchored across the Great Lakes. Weak easterly flow on southern \nperiphery of this high will allow for weak low level thermal \nadvections through the weekend with only a slight daily moderation \nin temperatures to the lower 70s for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nThe quiet weather will continue into the early next week as next \nupstream short wave still appears to be get dampened in background \nupper ridging. Prospects for more of a full latitude eastern Pacific \ntrough still look on track for the Tuesday-early Thursday. However, \nsome fairly large timing discrepancies persist among the various \ndeterministic/ensemble guidance with operational GFS being a \nfast outlier, even compared to GEFS. A focused period of higher \nPoPs will likely be needed at some point for some period early \nto middle of next week as forecast confidence in timing begins \nto increase.",
